1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,220
PART 3 Cathy: OK, Graham

2
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:07,650
so let's check we both know what we're supposed to be doing.

3
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:08,560
Graham: OK.

4
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:11,118
Cathy: So, for the university's open day,

5
00:00:11,118 --> 00:00:16,600
we have to plan a display on British life and literature in the mid-19th century.

6
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:17,460
Graham: That's right.

7
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:21,660
But we'll have some people to help us find the materials and set it up,

8
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:24,460
remember. For the moment, we just need to plan it.

9
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:29,500
Cathy: Good. So have you gathered who's expected to come and see the display?

10
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:34,110
Is it for the people studying English, or students from other departments?

11
00:00:34,110 --> 00:00:35,600
I'm not clear about it.

12
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:36,600
Graham: Nor me.

13
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:40,916
That was how it used to be, but it didn't attract many people,

14
00:00:40,916 --> 00:00:44,000
so this year it's going to be part of an open day

15
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,560
to raise the university's profile.

16
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:48,760
It'll be publicized in the city

17
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:53,290
to encourage people to come and find out something of what goes on here.

18
00:00:53,290 --> 00:00:56,822
And it's included in the information that's sent to people

19
00:00:56,822 --> 00:01:00,000
who are considering applying to study here next year.

20
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,610
Cathy: Presumably some current students and lecturers will come?

21
00:01:03,610 --> 00:01:08,920
Graham: I would imagine so, but we've been told to concentrate on the other categories of people.

22
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:14,190
Cathy: Right. We don't have to cover the whole range of 19th-century literature, do we?

23
00:01:14,190 --> 00:01:20,000
Graham: No, it's entirely up to us. I suggest just using Charles Dickens.

24
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,280
Cathy: That's a good idea. Most people have heard of him

25
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:26,237
and have probably read some of his novels,

26
00:01:26,237 --> 00:01:31,490
or seen films based on them, so that's a good lead-in to life in his time.

27
00:01:31,490 --> 00:01:37,244
Graham: Exactly. And his novels show the awful conditions that most people had to live in, don't they:

28
00:01:37,244 --> 00:01:40,440
he wanted to shock people into doing something about it.

29
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:43,770
Cathy: Did he do any campaigning, other than writing?

30
00:01:43,770 --> 00:01:47,837
Graham: Yes, he campaigned for education and other social reforms,

31
00:01:47,837 --> 00:01:53,530
and gave talks, but I'm inclined to ignore that and focus on the novels.

32
00:01:53,530 --> 00:01:56,930
Cathy: Yes, I agree.

33
00:01:56,930 --> 00:02:03,530
Cathy: OK, so now shall we think about a topic linked to each novel?

34
00:02:03,530 --> 00:02:09,290
Graham: Yes. I've printed out a list of Dickens's novels in the order they were published,

35
00:02:09,290 --> 00:02:12,530
in the hope you'd agree to focus on him!

36
00:02:12,530 --> 00:02:15,450
Cathy: You're lucky I did agree! Let's have a look.

37
00:02:15,450 --> 00:02:23,000
OK, the first was The Pickwick Papers, published in 1836.

38
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,360
It was very successful when it came out

39
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:29,180
wasn't it, and was adapted for the theatre straight away.

40
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:31,100
Graham: There's an interesting point, though

41
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:34,758
that there's a character who keeps falling asleep,

42
00:02:34,758 --> 00:02:39,140
and that medical condition was named after the book - Pickwickian Syndrome.

43
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:42,680
Cathy: Oh, so why don't we use that as the topic

44
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:45,210
and include some quotations from the novel?

45
00:02:45,210 --> 00:02:51,550
Graham: Right. Next is Oliver Twist. There's a lot in the novel about poverty.

46
00:02:51,550 --> 00:02:54,000
But maybe something less obvious...

47
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,500
Cathy: Well Oliver is taught how to steal, isn't he?

48
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:03,960
We could use that to illustrate the fact that very few children went to school,

49
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:07,600
particularly not poor children, so they learnt in other ways.

50
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:10,500
Graham: Good idea. What's next?

51
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:17,060
Cathy: Maybe Nicholas Nickleby. Actually he taught in a really cruel school, didn't he?

52
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:23,260
Graham: That's right. But there's also the company of touring actors that Nicholas joins.

53
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:28,440
We could do something on theaters and other amusements of the time.

54
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:31,260
We don't want only the bad things, do we?

55
00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:32,460
Cathy: OK.

56
00:03:32,460 --> 00:03:37,180
Graham: What about Martin Chuzzlewit? He goes to the USA, doesn't he?

57
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:41,520
Cathy: Yes, and Dickens himself had been there a year before

58
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:44,720
and drew on his experience there in the novel.

59
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:49,380
Graham: I wonder, though... The main theme is selfishness

60
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:54,000
so we could do something on social justice?

61
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,500
No, too general, let's keep to your idea - I think it would work well.

62
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:00,940
Cathy: He wrote Bleak House next

63
00:04:01,160 --> 00:04:04,420
that's my favourite of his novels. Graham: Yes, mine too.

64
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:07,690
His satire of the legal system is pretty powerful.

65
00:04:07,690 --> 00:04:11,000
Cathy: That's true, but think about Esther, the heroine.

66
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,500
As a child she lives with someone she doesn't know is her aunt

67
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:16,450
who treats her very badly.

68
00:04:16,450 --> 00:04:19,360
Then she's very happy living with her guardian

69
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:22,160
and he puts her in charge of the household.

70
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:27,223
And at the end she gets married and her guardian gives her and her husband a house,

71
00:04:27,223 --> 00:04:29,010
where of course they're very happy.

72
00:04:29,010 --> 00:04:30,690
Graham: Yes, I like that.

73
00:04:30,690 --> 00:04:35,100
Cathy: What shall we take next? Little Dorrit?

74
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:38,760
Old Mr Dorrit has been in a debtors' prison for years...

75
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:41,370
Graham: So was Dickens's father, wasn't he?

76
00:04:41,370 --> 00:04:42,610
Cathy: That's right.

77
00:04:42,610 --> 00:04:47,439
Graham: What about focusing on the part when Mr Dorrit inherits a fortune,

78
00:04:47,439 --> 00:04:50,410
and he starts pretending he's always been rich?

79
00:04:50,410 --> 00:04:51,630
Cathy: Good idea.

80
00:04:51,630 --> 00:04:58,230
Graham: OK, so next we need to think about what materials we want to illustrate each issue.

81
00:04:58,230 --> 00:05:02,000
That's going to be quite hard.
